Step 2: Washing the Fabric Samples

Each piece was washed thoroughly with clean water to remove dust or residues. This ensured that subsequent treatments would act only on the fibers. Establishing a clean baseline is crucial for accurate observations.

Step 3: Heating Water

A pot of water was brought to a boil to prepare for the mordant and dye treatments. Hot water helps activate chemical reactions in the fabrics. Consistent temperature ensures uniform treatment across samples.

Step 4: Preparing the Mordant

A measured amount of alum (potassium aluminum sulfate) was set aside for the treatment. Accurate measurement guarantees reproducibility. The mordant is key to altering fiber structure and texture.

Step 5: Dissolving the Alum

A portion of the boiling water was poured over the alum to dissolve it. Dissolving ensures even distribution in the treatment bath. This step prepares the solution for effective fiber interaction.

Step 6: Preparing the Alum Bath

The dissolved alum solution was added to the boiling water to form a uniform bath. A consistent concentration allows reliable comparison. The bath creates the environment for chemical changes in the linen.

Step 7: Mordant Treatment

Two of the linen pieces, initially white, were dipped into the alum bath. This step introduces chemical interaction with the fibers. Only part of the samples were treated to maintain control vs. experiment comparison.

Step 8: Overnight Soak

The fabrics were left submerged overnight to allow full penetration of the alum. Extended exposure ensures the fibers fully absorb the chemical. This mimics real-world processing conditions.

Step 9: Drying the Treated Linen

The treated pieces were removed from the bath and hung on a clothesline to dry. Air drying stabilizes the chemical treatment in the fibers. Consistent drying conditions allow fair observation of changes.

Step 10: Observing and Labeling Samples

After drying, the alum-treated fabrics were soft, light, and flexible. Each piece was labeled A* and B* (treated) and A and B (untreated) for clarity. Labeling ensures accurate tracking in later steps.

Step 11: Preparing Hibiscus Dye

Dried hibiscus petals were simmered in preheated water for 15–20 minutes to extract natural pigment. The process created a red-wine colored dye solution. Proper extraction ensures sufficient color for testing.

Step 12: Dyeing with Hibiscus

The alum-treated piece A* was dipped into the hibiscus solution. After drying, softness and flexibility remained unchanged. This confirmed that alum, not the dye, dominates tactile properties.

Step 13: Repeating with Onion Skin Dye

The process was repeated using onion skin dye on the alum-treated fabrics. The color changed, but texture and comfort remained the same. This repetition verified reproducibility and strengthened conclusions.

Step 14: Initial Conclusion

Alum treatment significantly improves linen’s texture, flexibility, and comfort. Natural dyes alone had minimal impact. The result shows that chemical processing, not colorants, dictates tactile properties.

Step 15: Confirming Post-Dyeing

Dyeing the alum-treated fabrics with hibiscus and onion skins did not alter tactile improvements. The effect remained soft, light, and flexible. This emphasizes that mordanting is the most influential factor in fabric processing.
